+## Caveats
+
+- This is Track B, not evidence that Roman domination or efficient domination is easy or hard on the generated distribution. A short Python scan solves the declared symbolic language in about a second.
+- The instance is an exact implicit graph, not an expanded adjacency list. The product at shipping size has about \(5\times10^{11}\) vertices; the polynomial Cayley presentation and hyperplane witness are essential to keeping posing and checking finite.
+- `P(random guess)` is uniform over pairs of normalized projective directions. It removes zero vectors and scalar duplicates, but it is not a prior over arbitrary Roman functions. The exact density applies only to the certificate language explicitly requested by the statement.
+- The adversary panel tried coefficient-energy outliers, coordinate projection, linear-term and leading-term ansatzes, and 256 random restarts. It did not run SAT/ILP or exact cover on the expanded graph, which is infeasible to materialize. The strongest applicable symbolic scan is disclosed as the successful Track B reference algorithm.
+- The canonical key handles ambient invertible linear-coordinate changes (tested through generators), parameter scaling, factor exchange, and compositions. It may fail to identify an exotic nonlinear isomorphism or a set-equivalent polynomial reparameterization outside this generated symmetry group; full Cayley-graph isomorphism is not attempted.
+- There may be multiple valid hyperplane normals on some seeds. `verify` accepts all of them, and G5 counts them exactly at the shipping measurement seed. The recorded shipping instance had one valid direction per factor; the demo has four valid pairs.
+- Most importantly, the required oracle hardness evidence is missing because of external quota exhaustion. Do not treat `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="easy"` as a completed release decision until the three harness arms produce scored attempts.
